const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Book Editing","serviceType":"Editing service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/book-editing/","description":"Scholarly book editing with a personalised list of 3–5 publishers, a proposal per press, indexing and permissions. $0.021 a word at 80,000 words.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What does professional book editing include?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Four editing levels and, for an academic book, a chain of work around them that is not editing at all.\n\nThe four levels: developmental editing deals “with content, organization, and genre considerations”; line editing works “at the sentence or paragraph level… improving the language and style”; copyediting involves “correcting spelling, grammar, usage, and punctuation, checking cross-references, and preparing the style sheets”; proofreading compares “the latest stage of the project to the previous one”.\n\nThe chain around them is what makes a book service a book service: a personalised list of three to five publishers, a proposal written to each press’s own published specification, a showcase chapter, permissions clearance, indexing against proofs, and the submission managed."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do COPE and the ICMJE apply to academic books?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The ICMJE is clear-cut: its title ends “…Publication of Scholarly Work in Medical Journals”, and its audience is authors submitting to ICMJE member journals. Books appear nowhere in its scope.\n\nCOPE needs a narrower answer: its membership categories and Core Practices are framed around peer-reviewed journals, and the only book-focused item on its site is a member discussion of 11 December 2024, marked as not formal COPE advice. Journal-framed, then — not a rule that books are excluded.\n\nWhat governs a book: the publisher’s contract, the commissioning editor, and at a university press peer review and editorial board approval, with AUPresses’ Best Practices for Peer Review of Scholarly Books, second edition September 2022."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I turn my thesis into a book?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"By removing the parts written for examiners — most of the work, and structural rather than linguistic.\n\nThree moves. The literature review shrinks drastically — on our sample, 11,000 words became about 2,000, the rest distributed into chapters where it does work. The methods chapter usually becomes an appendix. And the hedging comes out: 341 instances on that manuscript, protective in a viva, timid in a book.\n\nCommissioning editors recognise an unrevised thesis instantly — one press in our sample asked explicitly how the book differs from the thesis. The restrictions on what an editor may do to a thesis govern a thesis under examination. Once it is over, developmental and substantive editing are permissible."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does book editing cost?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Per word, with a volume discount that grows with length — 25% above 25,000 words, 40% above 60,000 and 50% above 120,000.\n\nAt 80,000 words that is $0.021 a word for copyediting ($1,680), $0.030 for line editing, $0.060 for developmental and $0.015 for proofreading. Edited collections add 20%. Indexing is $0.020 a word with a $450 minimum, permissions clearance is $400 plus the rights holders’ own fees, and the publisher chain is $1,200.\n\nPermissions fees charged by rights holders are yours and are not included in the $400, because we cannot know them before we ask."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"When should I start permissions, and how long do they take?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Before the editing, and longer than you expect. Every figure, image, map, long quotation or verse extract you did not create needs its copyright status established and permission obtained.\n\nOn timing we can cite rather than estimate: PLSclear, the UK permissions service, advises allowing six to eight weeks and notes some publishers take longer. Those are per request, and they run in parallel only if you start them together.\n\nThe reason to start early is the consequence of a refusal. On our sample, twenty-one items over eight weeks: two refused, one priced beyond budget, four never answered. One contract term worth checking at signature: in most academic book contracts, clearing permissions and paying the fees is the author’s responsibility."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"When can the index be made, and does my book need to be open access?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The index can only be made against typeset proofs, because it needs final page numbers. A publisher’s deadline is commonly two to three weeks from proofs. Anyone offering an index earlier is describing a concordance. Ours is $0.020 a word with a $450 minimum.\n\nOn open access: books sit outside most of the architecture built for journals. cOAlition S’s separate statement of 2 September 2021 made five recommendations, not requirements. UKRI is the live mandate: a monograph, chapter or edited collection published on or after 1 January 2024 acknowledging UKRI funding must be free to view and download within twelve months, under a CC licence, CC BY preferred, with four exemptions."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Editing and translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Editing and Translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/book-editing/"}]}];

